  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "The sweet «c» and the hard «c»",
   "texto": [
    "In front of **a, o, u**, the «c» sounds strong / k /, as in Spanish: **casa**, **come**, **ascolta**.",
    "In front of **e, i**, the «c» sounds sweet / tswana /, exactly as the Spanish «ch»: **cena** (read «chena»), **cinema** (read «chínema»), **dieci** (read «diechi»).",
    "To keep the sound strong / k / ante e, i, a mute «h» is interspersed: **chi** (read «ki»), **che** (read «ke»), **amiche** (read «amike»)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "The combinations of ciu, ciu, ciu",
   "texto": [
    "When you see **cia**, **cio** or **ciu**, the «i» is not pronounced as an independent vowel: it only serves to indicate that the «c» sounds sweet as «ch». So **ciao** is read «chao» and **cioccolato** is read «chocolato»."
   ]
